          "title": "1.2 Factors of Production: Land, Labour, Capital, Enterprise",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/5872353/pexels-photo-5872353.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RSyvcANRaOE",
          "content": "Definition:\nFactors of production (also known as economic resources or inputs) are the scarce resources used in the production process to create goods and services that satisfy human wants. They are the building blocks of economic activity.\n\nEconomists traditionally classify factors of production into four main categories:\n\nLand:\n\nDefinition: In economics, \"land\" refers to all natural resources provided by nature that are used in the production process. It is not just the physical surface of the earth.\n\nIncludes:\n\nPhysical land area (for farming, building sites).\n\nMineral deposits (oil, gas, coal, metals).\n\nWater resources (rivers, lakes, oceans).\n\nForests and timber.\n\nAgricultural fertility of the soil.\n\nClimate, sunlight, air.\n\nFisheries and wildlife.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nFixed Supply (Inelastic Supply): The total amount of land (natural resources) is largely fixed and cannot be easily increased by human effort, although its productivity can be improved.\n\nGift of Nature: It is a natural endowment, not man-made.\n\nImmobile: Land (in the physical sense) cannot be moved from one place to another.\n\nSubject to Law of Diminishing Returns: As more units of other factors (like labor and capital) are applied to a fixed amount of land, eventually the marginal output will start to decrease.\n\nHeterogeneous (Varies in Quality): Different pieces of land vary in fertility, location, and mineral content.\n\nReward (Income) for Land: Rent is the payment made for the use of land (or any natural resource).\n\nLabour:\n\nDefinition: All human effort, both physical and mental, that is directed towards the production of goods and services.\n\nIncludes: The work done by factory workers, farmers, teachers, doctors, managers, engineers, artists, etc.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nHuman Factor: Labour is inseparable from the laborer.\n\nPerishable: Labor cannot be stored; if a worker is idle for a day, that day's labor is lost forever.\n\nMobile (to some extent): Laborers can move from one occupation to another (occupational mobility) or from one geographical location to another (geographical mobility), though there are often barriers.\n\nHeterogeneous (Varies in Skill and Efficiency): Labor differs in terms of skill, education, training, and productivity. This is often referred to as human capital.\n\nActive Factor of Production: Labour actively uses other factors like land and capital.\n\nSupply of labor depends on population size, age structure, participation rates, education, and skills.\n\nReward (Income) for Labour: Wages and Salaries are the payments made for labor services.\n\nCapital:\n\nDefinition: In economics, \"capital\" refers to man-made goods (produced means of production) that are used to produce other goods and services. It is wealth that is used to create further wealth. It is distinct from financial capital (money).\n\nIncludes:\n\nPhysical Capital: Machinery, tools, equipment, buildings (factories, offices), infrastructure (roads, bridges), vehicles used in production.\n\nWorking Capital (Stock/Inventory): Raw materials, semi-finished goods, and finished goods held by businesses.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nMan-Made Factor: Produced by human effort using other resources.\n\nDurable (usually): Can be used repeatedly in production over a period of time (except working capital, which is consumed).\n\nSubject to Depreciation: Value of capital goods decreases over time due to wear and tear or obsolescence.\n\nMobile (often): Capital goods can usually be moved or their ownership transferred.\n\nIncreases Productive Capacity: Aids labor and makes production more efficient and large-scale.\n\nIts creation involves saving (postponing current consumption) and investment.\n\nReward (Income) for Capital: Interest is the payment made for the use of capital. (Note: \"Profit\" can also be seen as a return to capital invested by owners, but here interest refers to return on borrowed capital or the opportunity cost of using owned capital).\n\nEnterprise (Entrepreneurship):\n\nDefinition: The specialized human skill or ability of an entrepreneur to organize and combine the other factors of production (land, labor, capital) effectively to produce goods and services, and to undertake the risks and uncertainties involved in the business venture.\n\nFunctions of an Entrepreneur (recap from Commerce Chapter 11):\n\nInitiation: Conceiving the business idea and taking the initiative to start the venture.\n\nOrganization and Coordination: Bringing together and coordinating the other factors of production.\n\nDecision-Making: Making crucial decisions about what, how, and for whom to produce, and other operational and strategic matters.\n\nInnovation: Introducing new products, new methods of production, new markets, or new forms of organization.\n\nRisk-Bearing and Uncertainty-Bearing: Assuming the financial and other risks associated with the business, the outcomes of which are uncertain.\n\nCharacteristics: (As discussed in Commerce Chapter 11: vision, risk-taking, innovation, leadership, etc.)\n\nReward (Income) for Enterprise: Profit is the reward for successful entrepreneurship and risk-taking. If the venture fails, the entrepreneur may suffer a loss.\n\nThese four factors are essential for any production activity. The way they are combined and allocated determines the type and quantity of goods and services an economy can produce.",
