          "title": "2.2 Price Elasticity of Demand and Supply, Cross and Income Elasticities",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/19867469/pexels-photo-19867469.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HHcblIxiAAk",
          "content": "Elasticity is a measure of the **responsiveness or sensitivity** of one variable to a change in another variable. In economics, we are often interested in how quantity demanded or supplied responds to changes in price or other factors.\n\nPrice Elasticity of Demand (PED or E_d):\n*   **Definition:** Measures the **responsiveness of the quantity demanded of a good to a change in its own price**, *ceteris paribus*.\n*   **Formula:**\n    PED = (% Change in Quantity Demanded) / (% Change in Price)\n    *   % Change in Quantity Demanded = [(New Q_d - Old Q_d) / Old Q_d] × 100% (or midpoint method for more accuracy over a range).\n    *   % Change in Price = [(New P - Old P) / Old P] × 100% (or midpoint method).\n*   **Interpretation of Values:**\n    *   PED is usually **negative** (due to the law of demand – price and quantity move in opposite directions). However, economists often refer to the **absolute value** (ignoring the minus sign) when classifying elasticity.\n    *   **Elastic Demand (PED > 1):** Quantity demanded is very responsive to price changes. A given percentage change in price leads to a larger percentage change in quantity demanded. The demand curve is relatively flatter.\n        *   *Example:* Luxury goods, goods with many substitutes.\n    *   **Inelastic Demand (PED < 1):** Quantity demanded is not very responsive to price changes. A given percentage change in price leads to a smaller percentage change in quantity demanded. The demand curve is relatively steeper.\n        *   *Example:* Necessities (food, medicine), goods with few substitutes, addictive goods (cigarettes).\n    *   **Unit Elastic Demand (PED = 1):** Quantity demanded changes by the same percentage as the price.\n    *   **Perfectly Inelastic Demand (PED = 0):** Quantity demanded does not change at all regardless of price changes. Demand curve is a vertical line. (Very rare, e.g., life-saving drug with no substitutes for a desperate patient).\n    *   **Perfectly Elastic Demand (PED = ∞):** Consumers are willing to buy an infinite amount at a particular price, but none at a price even slightly higher. Demand curve is a horizontal line. (Theoretical, applies to individual firms in perfect competition).\n*   **Relationship between PED and Total Revenue (TR = Price × Quantity):**\n    *   **Elastic Demand (PED > 1):** If price increases, TR decreases. If price decreases, TR increases.\n    *   **Inelastic Demand (PED < 1):** If price increases, TR increases. If price decreases, TR decreases.\n    *   **Unit Elastic Demand (PED = 1):** If price changes, TR remains unchanged.\n    This relationship is crucial for firms when making pricing decisions.\n*   **Determinants of Price Elasticity of Demand:**\n    1.  **Availability of Substitutes:** The more (and closer) substitutes available, the more elastic the demand (consumers can easily switch).\n    2.  **Nature of the Good (Necessity vs. Luxury):** Necessities tend to have inelastic demand; luxuries tend to have elastic demand.\n    3.  **Proportion of Income Spent on the Good:** Goods that constitute a small fraction of consumer income tend to have inelastic demand (e.g., salt). Goods that take a large proportion have more elastic demand.\n    4.  **Time Horizon:** Demand tends to be more elastic in the long run than in the short run, as consumers have more time to find substitutes or adjust their behavior.\n    5.  **Definition of the Market:** Narrowly defined markets (e.g., a specific brand of coffee) have more elastic demand than broadly defined markets (e.g., coffee in general).\n    6.  **Addictiveness/Habit-forming nature:** Addictive goods tend to have inelastic demand.\n\nPrice Elasticity of Supply (PES or E_s):\n*   **Definition:** Measures the **responsiveness of the quantity supplied of a good to a change in its own price**, *ceteris paribus*.\n*   **Formula:**\n    PES = (% Change in Quantity Supplied) / (% Change in Price)\n*   **Interpretation of Values:**\n    *   PES is usually **positive** (due to the law of supply – price and quantity supplied move in the same direction).\n    *   **Elastic Supply (PES > 1):** Quantity supplied is very responsive to price changes. Supply curve is relatively flatter.\n    *   **Inelastic Supply (PES < 1):** Quantity supplied is not very responsive to price changes. Supply curve is relatively steeper.\n    *   **Unit Elastic Supply (PES = 1).**\n    *   **Perfectly Inelastic Supply (PES = 0):** Quantity supplied does not change regardless of price. Supply curve is vertical (e.g., supply of rare paintings, land in short run).\n    *   **Perfectly Elastic Supply (PES = ∞):** Producers are willing to supply an infinite amount at a particular price, but none below that price. Supply curve is horizontal.\n*   **Determinants of Price Elasticity of Supply:**\n    1.  **Time Horizon:** Supply is more elastic in the long run than in the short run. In the short run, firms may not be able to easily change production levels. In the long run, they can adjust capacity, build new plants, or new firms can enter/exit.\n    2.  **Availability of Inputs/Spare Capacity:** If a firm has spare production capacity or can easily obtain inputs, supply will be more elastic.\n    3.  **Mobility of Factors of Production:** If factors can be easily shifted from producing other goods, supply will be more elastic.\n    4.  **Nature of the Good (Storability):** Goods that can be easily stored tend to have more elastic supply (producers can release from stock if price rises).\n    5.  **Complexity of Production:** Goods with complex production processes tend to have less elastic supply in the short run.\n\nCross-Price Elasticity of Demand (XED or E_xy):\n*   **Definition:** Measures the **responsiveness of the quantity demanded of one good (Good X) to a change in the price of another good (Good Y)**, *ceteris paribus*.\n*   **Formula:**\n    XED = (% Change in Quantity Demanded of Good X) / (% Change in Price of Good Y)\n*   **Interpretation of Values:**\n    *   **Substitutes (XED > 0, Positive):** If the price of Good Y increases, quantity demanded of Good X increases (consumers switch to Good X).\n        *   *Example:* If price of coffee rises, demand for tea increases.\n        *   The larger the positive XED, the closer the substitutes.\n    *   **Complements (XED < 0, Negative):** If the price of Good Y increases, quantity demanded of Good X decreases (as they are used together).\n        *   *Example:* If price of petrol rises, demand for large cars might fall.\n        *   The larger the negative XED (in absolute terms), the stronger the complementarity.\n    *   **Unrelated Goods (XED ≈ 0):** A change in the price of Good Y has little or no effect on the quantity demanded of Good X.\n\nIncome Elasticity of Demand (YED or E_i):\n*   **Definition:** Measures the **responsiveness of the quantity demanded of a good to a change in consumer income**, *ceteris paribus*.\n*   **Formula:**\n    YED = (% Change in Quantity Demanded) / (% Change in Consumer Income)\n*   **Interpretation of Values:**\n    *   **Normal Goods (YED > 0, Positive):** Demand increases as income increases.\n        *   **Necessities (0 < YED < 1):** Demand increases less than proportionally to income (e.g., basic food). Income inelastic.\n        *   **Luxuries (YED > 1):** Demand increases more than proportionally to income (e.g., sports cars, foreign holidays). Income elastic.\n    *   **Inferior Goods (YED < 0, Negative):** Demand decreases as income increases (consumers switch to better quality goods).\n\nElasticities are vital tools for businesses in making pricing and production decisions, and for governments in analyzing the impact of taxes and subsidies.",
